olynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-9,$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-9}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{9}{2}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(9)$ is a factor of $-9$ and the denominator $(2)$ is a factor of $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083892 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083892 #edb/ref 1069 "q-6676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083892 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083892 #edb/ref 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-3x+12?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083892 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b3698582-22d9-54f7-afa9-667b63cf2255" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083892 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $12$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }12}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{1}{2}$ does not take the above form. The denominator $(2)$ is not a factor of $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084136 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084136 #edb/ref 1061 20965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084136 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5b104caf-8c0c-5010-8dfa-bbe8604d3403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084136 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084137 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084137 #edb/ref 1061 20966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084137 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c6a84a8-f379-51e0-8e5f-3ee50cce2374" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084137 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084240 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084240 #edb/ref 1061 27328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084240 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ce34fd62-4f9c-52b9-ba64-1e297909071e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084240 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084241 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084241 #edb/ref 1061 27362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084241 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b52b9e4-cd28-50db-a889-bf6f2fd5e07e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084241 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084245 #edb/ref 1061 27497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084245 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1548a389-5cb1-572b-abf2-33279abef94e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084245 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084456 #edb/ref 1064 "The **rational roots theorem** (or **rational zeros theorem**) describes the rational roots of a polynomial with integer coefficients. The statement of the theorem is as follows:\n\n*Suppose that the rational number $\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{p}}}{\\color{red}{q}},$ in lowest terms, is a root of a polynomial with integer coefficients. Then $\\color{blue}p$ must be a factor of the polynomial's constant term, and $\\color{red}q$ must be a factor of its leading coefficient.*\n\nIn other words, given a polynomial with integer coefficients, any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = {\\color{red}{8}}x^3+12x^2+6x+{\\color{blue}{2}}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the rational roots theorem, any rational root of $f(x)$ must have a numerator that is a factor of $\\color{blue}{2}$ (that is, either $\\color{blue} \\pm1$ or $\\color{blue} \\pm2$) and a denominator that's a factor of $\\color{red} 8$ (${\\color{red} \\pm1},$ ${\\color{red} \\pm2},$ ${\\color{red} \\pm4},$ or $\\color{red} \\pm8$). \n\nAt first glance, it may seem like we have $16$ possible rational roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, if we reduce the above fractions to lowest form, we see that there are some duplicates, which we cancel out:\n\n![](images/91/91d3a123202f03ecec2d568548b5964fdc941e77d9edbad8ab386954bb046740.svg) \n\nRemoving the duplicates, we have ten *possible* rational roots. We list them from least to greatest in absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm 1, & \\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Watch out!** The above roots are only *possible* rational roots. They are *not* guaranteed to be roots of our polynomial $f(x).$ The only guarantee is that if $f(x)$ has any roots that are rational numbers, they are included in the list of numbers above. Here, it turns out that the only actual root among the possibilities is $-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084456 #edb/ref 1065 3729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084456 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "85dfd162-8cfc-59dd-8f7d-d66bbde1873c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084456 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234452 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234452 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83863" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234452 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234452 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-5,-1),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234452 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38a31983-df9c-53f1-81ab-91f00755216b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234452 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $8$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }8}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 4,\\pm 8\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-5,-1),$ are $-4$ and $-2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-10$ | $8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-4$ | $12$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+4)({x}^{2}-3x+2) \\\\  & =(x+4)(x-1)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-4,1,2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-4+1+2=-1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269334 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269334 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269334 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0717f98a-a00b-5e12-b164-bbe07b4506d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271195 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271195 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271195 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dc0ed403-118c-5e8a-b288-f4a9fdf647c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271433 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271433 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271433 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f6b8d9d8-b733-55b7-8453-d96684728e42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271459 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271459 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271459 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fa96b2fb-d03d-5d55-ae12-d85627604770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271496 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271496 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271496 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fe5ffb9c-df8a-54bf-b04e-3951b89b2186" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271521 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271521 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271521 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271521 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271521 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271521 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271521 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271521 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186269334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271521 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "08b318fe-5549-5f3d-b17c-a826bb2d784c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272279 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272279 #edb/ref 1069 "q-14142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272279 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272279 #edb/ref 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x)=6{x}^{4}-15{x}^{2}-2x+5,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272279 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0caeff2-4a23-5ec8-b046-51ecdd035f70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272279 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{5}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 5, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{6},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\,\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300379 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300379 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300379 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5000c0c0-6878-5962-9058-447048182357" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300476 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300476 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300476 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "65f3eacd-9a6b-543d-b62c-562bfd26d7cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300660 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300660 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300660 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8a1e8c4f-3bd0-5bf1-8c22-f1c