5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302076 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302076 #edb/ref 1071 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-3x+2$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94f4999f-cddb-56aa-8dc1-690a88c2e2d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302076 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2$, $\\pm 4$, and $\\pm 8.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\text{ } \\\\ \\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $10$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 5=10.$)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302144 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302144 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302144 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302144 #edb/ref 1069 "q-323610" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302144 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302144 #edb/ref 1071 "The polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-10x-8$ has one of its roots in the interval $(3,5).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302144 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44f388d6-5078-5e1a-a0fb-9aeba444134a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302144 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(3,5)$ is $4.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-10$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $12$ | $8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-4)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}+3x+2).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-4)({x}^{2}+3x+2) \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+1)(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-2,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324250 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324250 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324250 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21be555d-ccf5-5281-af53-b2f20b2ad18f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324764 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324764 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324764 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5ee070d1-a630-540b-987e-0ac74c34b1b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325160 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325160 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325160 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8beb16eb-e9fb-5629-a4f3-3b93148cab23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325461 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325461 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325461 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "af2f4add-ae51-5ca7-9041-e9840321cc19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325466 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325466 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325466 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "af66a0e6-8e66-5d0f-bc3a-7e64495ed0b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186324250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186324764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186325160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186325461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186325466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326256 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326256 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326256 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186324764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326256 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1386af21-b0ec-52ed-a2ae-90ae0f4d84fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326676 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326676 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38251" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326676 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326676 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-20{x}^{2}+31x-15$ has one of its roots in the interval $(1,2),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326676 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d5d77179-b48c-5827-b197-d34e1d977ab0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326676 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-15$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-15}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 3,\\pm 5,\\pm 15,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\pm \\frac{15}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\pm \\frac{3}{4},\\pm \\frac{5}{4},\\pm \\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(1,2),$ are $\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $4$ | $-20$ | $31$ | $-15$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $6$ | $-21$ | $15$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-14$ | $10$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-\\frac{3}{2})(4{x}^{2}-14x+10).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{3}{2})(4{x}^{2}-14x+10) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2{x}^{2}-7x+5) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2x-5)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},1.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}+1=5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333002 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333002 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333002 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "019ddcea-817d-5f44-a7b0-829d12525250" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333235 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333235 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333235 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ab08216-a85b-52d8-bfb5-9e9512ad46ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333265 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333265 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333265 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1f0c3209-4358-57e9-9922-daedefbb9777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333422 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333422 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333422 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2eaed666-d59c-5650-a6f4-1404e03715a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333477 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333477 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333477 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "351fac38-0a31-517e-9482-0406caca8c8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333522 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333522 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333522 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3916b332-b1bf-56dd-ba24-217edc46155a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333955 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333955 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333955 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "69ad4502-e492-59c7-a566-d06801