he polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }4}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $8$ does not take the above form. The value $8$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{8}{1},$ and the numerator $(8)$ is not a factor of $4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348711 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348711 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348711 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1e2401d9-a1a7-5505-97d5-f5f33069493a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349873 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349873 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349873 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a0d431b2-319d-53c4-a25c-fb923316f78f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350146 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350146 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350146 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bf57bdc6-ce16-5268-91e5-d3d687c3f190" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350418 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350418 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350418 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e07c502f-3e8e-5a88-8569-299a91e84ba8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350704 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350704 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350704 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ffba016b-9fa9-5f8a-9115-9f17867a5b3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350993 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186348711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350993 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350993 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350993 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350993 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350993 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350993 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350993 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186349873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350993 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a7672189-18d2-5df0-8883-48d7a14bb837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351526 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351526 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351526 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351526 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+5x-3$ has a root in the interval $(0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351526 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86dd84a3-806b-59bf-94a9-b6c66e0b00ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351526 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\pm 1,\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ are $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $1.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{1}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-3$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n|  | $2$ | $2$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-\\frac{1}{2})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{2})(2{x}^{2}+2x+6).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+2x+6$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-4(2)(6) \\\\  & =4-48 \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369147 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369147 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369147 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369147 #edb/ref 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+x+6?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369147 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff153540-97c0-598a-84af-1ded375d76ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369147 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{3}{4}$ does not take the above form. The denominator $(4)$ is not a factor of $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369148 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369148 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369148 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369148 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369148 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369148 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369148 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369148 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186369152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369148 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "70b789b7-b641-5d56-91db-ef1575144f19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369149 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369149 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369149 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3a0522a7-0507-5731-a4b7-8bba18622019" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369150 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369150 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369150 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8fde88cc-1dfe-551e-b505-43ea2c33d068" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369151 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369151 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369151 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "83ab7bbf-94b2-5cb5-9325-46ad92883dbd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369152 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369152 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369152 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a6bc4bf1-1c7f-5632-a344-3a92a856f8ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369153 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369153 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369153 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ba1d826c-ea85-5f67-b6de-9779918444e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370028 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370028 #edb/ref 1069 "q-321979" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370028 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370028 #edb/ref 1071 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-7x+30$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370028 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30c939ec-fbca-5126-a442-cf703484d532" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370028 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $30,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,\\pm 5,\\pm 6,\\pm 10,\\pm 15,\\pm 30.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $-1,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{5}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{6}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{10}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{15}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{30}{1}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are only $\\pm 1,$ these simplify to:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 3,\\text{ }\\pm 5,\\text{ }\\pm 6,\\text{ }\\pm 10,\\text{ }\\pm 15,\\text{ }\\pm 30.\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. \n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370029 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370029 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370029 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370029 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186370030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370029 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "72a7d874-45df-5575-bf0d-420b6a82e0bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370030 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370030 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186370030 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d58f1e42-9acf-5bdd-844b-065909d75d35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371160 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83734" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371160 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371160 #edb/ref 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=6{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-9x+4?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371160 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66ff8aa3-feb2-535d-bc98-e1c2676caf7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371160 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4,$ and the leading coefficient is $6.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }4}{\\text{factor of }6}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{2}{3}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(2)$ is a factor of $4$ and the denominator $(3)$ is a factor of $6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371161 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371161 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371161 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371161 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371161 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371161 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371161 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371161 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371161 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ec666cee-c0ee-5c03-a6cf-d2d22e402586" #edb/ref